# In-channel Call Panel

Sometimes a message thread just needs to become a call. The **call panel** lets a
channel spin up a live video room *in place*, so nobody has to context-switch to
a separate meeting surface.

## Same stack, embedded framing

The call panel isn't a second video implementation — it's the **same LiveKit
media stack** described in [Video Rooms](video-rooms.xmd), rendered inside the
channel instead of as a standalone room.

- Same grant flow (`POST /api/rtc/token` via `bridge-rtc`).
- Same self-hosted media server over loopback WebRTC.
- Same in-room features: active-speaker highlighting, raise-hand, device switching.

## Loaded on demand

> [!tip] The channel stays light until you call
> The call panel pulls in `livekit-client` through a **deep lazy import**, so the
> heavy WebRTC client stays out of the warm chunk. A channel you only ever type
> in never pays the cost of the video code.

```text
 Channel view
 ├─ message column      ← always mounted, lightweight
 └─ [ Start call ] ─────► call panel mounts
                          └─► lazy-loads livekit-client
                          └─► joins the room, tiles appear inline
```

## When to use which

| You want… | Use |
|:----------|:----|
| A quick call tied to the conversation | **Call panel** (this page) |
| A dedicated, full-surface room | **Video room** ([Video Rooms](video-rooms.xmd)) |

Both connect to the same room infrastructure, so participants can be in the same
call regardless of which entry point they used.

> [!note] One media plane
> Whether you join from the panel or the full room, you're on the **same
> self-hosted LiveKit server**. The distinction is purely how the video is framed
> in the UI — not a separate backend.

## Recap

- [x] Video without leaving the channel
- [x] Reuses the LiveKit grant + media stack
- [x] Deep lazy import keeps the warm bundle small
- [x] Interoperates with the standalone video room
